"relationship":"same_topic",
"comparison":"Both Citigroup and Bank of America disclose the cash flow implications of reclassifying loans from held-for-investment (or similar lending assets) to held-for-sale or foreclosed properties. Citi specifies that while the loan moves to other assets on the balance sheet, the associated cash flow is reported in investing activities under 'proceeds from sales and securitizations of loans'. Similarly, BAC notes that reclassifying consumer real estate loans to foreclosed properties represents 'non-cash investing activities' which are 'not reflected in the Consolidated Statement of Cash Flows'. Both address how internal transfers or reclassifications of loan assets impact cash flow reporting, distinguishing these non-cash movements from actual cash inflows/outflows.",
"target_sentence_ids":["c_2024_8_P066_S002"],
"peer_sentence_ids":["bac_2024_8_P229_S004"]
},
{
"peer_ticker":"JPM",
"peer_disclosure_id":"jpm_2024_8_D314",
"relationship":"same_topic",
"comparison":"Both Citi and JPMorgan explicitly state that the reclassification of loans to held-for-sale is a non-cash transaction. Citi explains that when intent changes from HFI to HFS, the loan is reclassified but cash flows continue to be reported in investing activities, implying the reclassification event itself is not a cash flow event. JPMorgan is more direct, stating in a footnote that 'Reclassifications of loans to held-for-sale are non-cash transactions.' Both disclosures address the specific accounting treatment of the reclassification step itself in the context of cash flows.",
"target_sentence_ids":["c_2024_8_P066_S002"],
"peer_sentence_ids":["jpm_2024_8_P468_S001"]
},
{
"peer_ticker":"GS",
"peer_disclosure_id":"gs_2024_8_D215",
"relationship":"related_topic",
"comparison":"Citi and Goldman Sachs both classify loans into held-for-investment and held-for-sale categories, which is the structural prerequisite for the reclassification topic Citi discusses. Citi focuses on the cash flow reporting consequence of moving between these two states. GS defines the accounting measurement for these categories (amortized cost for HFI, lower of cost or fair value for HFS) but does not explicitly detail the specific cash flow line item behavior or non-cash nature of the reclassification event in the same manner as Citi. The comparison is on the shared loan classification framework, but the specific cash flow disclosure of the reclassification mechanism is unique to Citi in this set.",
"target_sentence_ids":["c_2024_8_P066_S002"],
"peer_sentence_ids":["gs_2024_8_P299_S001"]
}
